// Royal Coachman: John Haily's 1878 Coachman with a red silk band, in its dry dressing with white duck quill wings.

id royal-coachman
name "Royal Coachman"
type dry
imitates attractor
naturals mayfly-dun
difficulty advanced
species trout grayling
season may june july august september
origin originator="John Haily" year=1878 region="New York, USA"
description "A golden pheasant tippet tail, a body of peacock herl with a red floss band, upright white duck quill wings and a brown hackle."
history """
    The Coachman, a wet fly with a peacock body and white wings, is credited to Tom Bosworth,
    coachman to the British court in the 1830s. In 1878 John Haily, a professional tyer in New
    York, was asked for sturdier ones: he bound a band of red silk round the middle of the herl
    to stop it fraying and gave the fly a barred wood duck tail. Mary Orvis Marbury tells how
    her uncle L. C. Orvis, seeing how finely it was dressed, named it the Royal Coachman. It was
    first a wet fly and is mostly fished dry today, with a tippet tail; Theodore Gordon gave it
    fan wings, and in 1930 the hair-winged Quack Coachman led to the Royal Wulff. The name is
    also spelled Hailey; Ahrex reports an English origin from Collier.
    """
fishing """
    An attractor, fished dead drift as an ordinary dry when trout are not feeding on anything in
    particular, and the classic brook trout fly. The white wings make it easy to follow on the
    water. Quill wings split after a few fish, which is why many anglers carry the Royal Wulff
    instead.
    """
sources {
    "Wikipedia: Royal Coachman" url="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Coachman"
    "MidCurrent: The Royal Coachman (Ian Whitelaw)" url="https://midcurrent.com/?p=24126"
    "Fly Fishers International: Fly of the Month, the Coachman (Jim Abbs, 2000)" url="https://www.flyfishersinternational.org/Portals/0/FlyoftheMonth/PreviousIssues/2000-09%20Sept%20-%20Coachman.pdf?ver=2012-03-12-215818-573"
    "Fly Tying Guide: Royal Coachman Dry (archived)" url="http://web.archive.org/web/20250819001112/https://www.flytying.guide/patterns/royal-coachman-dry"
    "Ahrex Hooks: The Royal Coachman (Håkan Karsnäser)" url="https://ahrexhooks.com/2021/01/08/the-royal-coachman/"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://perfectflystore.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/royal_coachman.jpg" page="https://perfectflystore.com/product/royal-coachman-dry/" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view in the vise"
    "http://web.archive.org/web/20250819001112im_/https://www.flytying.guide/Media/uploads/2054/0fe0505b-ae32-410a-aa4e-4d9795f859f9.jpg?width=600&quality=80" page="http://web.archive.org/web/20250819001112/https://www.flytying.guide/patterns/royal-coachman-dry" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Near side view, from a little below"
}

sits hackle
hook dry-standard size=12
thread black size="8/0"

materials {
    wings duck-quill color=white label="White duck quill slips"
    tail golden-pheasant-tippet count=7
    butt peacock-herl strands=2
    band silk-floss color=red
    front peacock-herl strands=2
    hackle cock-hackle color=brown fibre=1.3 label="Coachman brown cock hackle"
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.9
    wrap-thread to=0.75
    // Sources: one shank long; the photos show 1.9 to 2.5 gapes above the shank.
    tie-in wings length=2.0 angle=85 spread=26 width=0.75 note="A slip a gape wide from a left and a right quill, curves out, tips forward; stood up and divided later."
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    // Sources: one shank; the photos show about 1.7 gapes.
    tie-in tail length=1.7 spread=20 note="Six to eight tippet barbs, tips level."
    tie-in butt
    wrap-thread to=0.16
    wind butt to=0.16 turns=4 note="Four or five turns."
    tie-off butt
    tie-in band
    // Read from the photos: the band is narrower than a third, in the rear half.
    wrap-thread to=0.4
    wind band to=0.4 turns=6 note="A smooth red band, a little narrower than the herl sections in many flies."
    tie-off band
    tie-in front
    wrap-thread to=0.58
    wind front to=0.58 turns=4
    tie-off front
    tie-in hackle
    wrap-thread to=0.92
    wind hackle to=0.9 turns=6 note="Behind the wings, then the wings pulled back and the rest in front."
    tie-off hackle
    whip-finish
}
